<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Henhouse API Portal</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/style.css">
</head>
<body>
  <main class="site-main">
    <article class="auth-card" style="max-width:40rem;">
      <h1>&#128273; Henhouse API Portal</h1>
      <p>Here is your freshly-issued <strong>guest</strong> token (signed HS256):</p>
      <pre style="white-space:pre-wrap; word-break:break-all;"><%= guestToken %></pre>
      <p style="color:var(--ink-soft); font-size:0.95rem;">
        Present it to the API and it'll greet you as a guest:
      </p>
      <pre>/c/token-of-trust/api?token=&lt;your token&gt;</pre>
      <p style="color:var(--ink-soft); font-size:0.95rem;">
        A JWT is just <code>base64url(header).base64url(payload).signature</code>. You can't fake
        the HS256 signature without the secret &mdash; but the verifier trusts whatever the
        <strong>header's <code>alg</code></strong> claims. Decode the three parts, think about what
        an attacker could set <code>alg</code> to, and rebuild a token whose payload says
        <code>"role":"admin"</code>.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:1.5rem;"><a href="/challenges/token-of-trust">&larr; Back to the challenge</a></p>
    </article>
  </main>
</body>
</html>
